+# Context Plane
+
+> **In one line:** The one door agents use to reach data, and the three things that stand at it.
+
+**You are here:** START HERE › Provenance › Context Plane
+**Audience:** 🟡 engineer · **Reads in:** ~3 min
+
+## The 30-second version
+
+Agents never open the data tables. They ask through a door. Two servers offer a fixed
+set of questions, a gateway checks who is asking and whether the rules allow it, and
+every ask is written down before it is answered.
+
+## The picture
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  GOLD["Gold views<br><i>agent-ready tables</i>"] --> EMCP["evidence-mcp<br><i>fixed evidence queries</i>"]
+  CMCP["controls-mcp<br><i>the rulebooks, machine-readable</i>"] --> GW
+  EMCP --> GW["Gateway<br><i>identity, policy, audit, forward</i>"]
+  GW --> OPA["Policy engine<br><i>allows or denies each call</i>"]
+  GW --> AUDIT["Audit log<br><i>every call, denials included</i>"]
+```
+
+## How it works
+
+1. **Gold views.** The small tables built for agents.
+2. **evidence-mcp.** Fixed, parameterized evidence queries scoped by system.
+3. **Gateway.** Verifies the token, asks the policy engine, writes the audit row, forwards.
+4. **Audit log.** Append-only; denials included.
+
+## The details
+
+The controls server serves the NIST SP 800-171 and SOC 2 catalogs in OSCAL. The policy
+engine is Open Policy Agent with Rego rules that default to deny.
+
+## Why it's built this way
+
+One door is what makes "every agent action is logged and policy-checked" a fact rather
+than a convention (BR-7, ADR-001, ADR-003).
+
